ice  so particularly i've been interested  in a beta cell replacement for the  treatment of type 1 diabetes  so on type 1 diabetes these patients  have  an autoimmune response to their beta  cells  which end up ends up destroying these  cells so they can no longer produce  insulin and so the current standard of  care for these  types of patients is they break their  finger they  monitor their blood glucose levels so  either manually inject  the amount of insulin that they're  needed or  there have been advances where you can  get continuous  glucose monitors and even insulin pumps  however it's been very difficult to  fully  recapitulate the dynamic uh kind of  glycemic control that's normally seen in  the body  so normally this is uh blood glucose  homeostasis  is maintained by the islets of langurons  which are  cell clusters that secrete  uh various hormones such as insulin  which will lower blood glucose levels  on glucagon which will raise blood  glucose levels  and then somatostatin and some other  hormones that uh  tight glycemic control  and so the body has evolved and these  eyelet cells have really evolved to  maintain  this tightly regulated blood glucose  levels which you can see  within this narrow range  that is highlighted by the the red  dashed lines  and using a continuous  glucose monitor of patients that have  undergoing  manual insulin injections you can see  but they're often outside of this range  and even when you use an insulin pump  that can try to you know precisely time  when to  inject this insulin they're still often  out of this range  and this is really being outside of  these  ranges is what leads to the long-term  complications and the increased  morbidity  associated with type 1 diabetes and so  i've really been focused on how can we  replace  this cellular control system to really  restore this  physiological glucose control  and so in my previous lab  i worked in a phase three clinical  eyelid transplantation where we would  take  a donor pancreas we would enzymatically  and mechanically digest the organ into  small tissue fragments  we would then isolate the islets and  infuse these  into the liver of type 1 diabetic  patients where they'd become  large and small capillaries and then  these cells would just live  in the patient's livers and so this  was very effective and it would restore  the dynamic and that physiological  blood glucose control we had some  patients that were influent independent  for over 10 years however there are some  drawbacks so  like i said this requires systemic  immunosuppression  and those drawbacks and there's also  a limited number of organs that can  treat all the diabetics  and so i've been working on  kind of a simple method of cell  encapsulation within  algebraic microbes so where the ios can  become  entrapped within these hydrogel spheres  and alginate is nice because it's very  cell friendly and it crosslinks  in the presence of divalent cations to  solidify this gel  around the eyelids  and so this strategy has worked very  well  in many diabetic mouse models  so here this red dash line any blood  glucose level below  this line is within the normal range  signifying that these animals are no  longer diabetic  and these encapsulated islets can really  function for  very long term up to a year in these  mice  and this has led to a small number of  clinical trials  with encapsulated human ios and these  same  kind of alginate based formulations that  were tested in these mice  however the human patients only  experience  transient eyelid graft function and very  little  therapeutic benefit and so one of the  main issues  is that investors investigators reported  clumping of the microcapsules adherence  to a mental tissue  and a strong fibrotic fibrotic response  of these ilo grafts and so in here i can  see this thick layer of  fibrosis surrounding the capsule in this  kind of dense ecm  and cellular network it really restricts  the diffusion of oxygen  and other nutrients that are important  for the cell survival  and ends up killing these islets  so kind of historically people have  debated  um is this an islet problem or is this a  material problem  um so isolated islets can vary in  quality depending on  the isolation process which is very  artisanal  and also just the uh or the organ donor  and like that the quality of  how they happen to pass away and some of  the issues surrounding  the organs  and then another potential cause for  this lack of functions that  this intraperitoneal transplantation  site in the abdominal cavity  uh it's been associated with low oxygen  levels which may not be ideal and maybe  this led to  this graph failure however in certain  romance rodent models  people have shown that there is a  foreign body response  to alginate-based formulations and this  has a consequence negative effect on  iowa graph function and so we wanted to  test this so  we took those same formulations that  were implanted  uh implemented in clinic clinical trials  we formulated them as empty beads so not  including  uh any islets and implanted them into a  higher order  uh animal species a non-human primate uh  that  perhaps is more similar to a human  patient  and so by one week we started to see  cells attached to the surface of the  microbeads and by four weeks these were  completely  uh embedded and in fibrotic tissue  so very uh similar to the reports  with the encapsulated ios  we looked at the different cell  populations that were responding to  these alginate-based formulations and  from cb11b  myeloid foreign body giant cells apple  smooth muscle actin  myofibroblasts and many of the salt  types that are associated with foreign  body responses to implantable materials  so these results really suggested that  the fundamental  problem was material based and not  necessarily with the ios themselves  so a very talented post-doc arturo vegas  at mit was screening  small molecule modifications to the  alginate backbone  to find alginate derivatives that could  inhibit this  fibrosis or this foreign body response  to materials  so of these 800 you found three  different formulations that could  prevent fibrosis and mice  and then these were shipped to chicago  where we could then test these  in the nhp model as we attested  the clinical algebras and so from these  three  formulations we found two that were the  most effective  at reducing fibrosis alginate spheres  so then we wanted to see how this  would protect allogeneic ios  however over the course of these  experiments we observed that we would  transplant these spheres  near the liver and the abdominal cavity  however over time they tend to descend  and settle within the douglas space in  the base of the pelvis  and so we really didn't want to form any  clumps because if you can think that  any cells in the center of these clumps  are going to be super far away from any  blood vessels  and that is going to restrict basically  similar to fibrosis  and lead to loss of viability so we  hypothesized that we could  place the encapsulated islets within  uh the bursa omentalis um so this is  this thin  uh membrane uh that tissue bilayer  basically  uh this hangs like an apron above your  intestines  and so we hypothesize that the friction  of those diesel mental  kind of folds and these tissue layers  will prevent the sedimentation of these  alginate spheres  transplantation technique  so here is a video of the  transplantation  technique so laparoscopically we make a  a small incision  uh in the greater momentum  then our encapsulated iots and spheres  are within a syringe and then they are  infused  um laparoscopically into the bursal  mentalis  and so here you can see that the  encapsulated eyelets  are within that tissue bilayer so it's a  little hard  it's a little hard to see but you can  see maybe like one capsule where  um it kind of goes underneath that fast  fat tissue and it's kind of in between  that that tissue by layer  so then f  so then after four weeks we wanted to  retrieve the ios first to observe  how they look within the intraperitoneal  space  and so we can see that they are well  distributed  within that tissue bilayer in the bursal  mentalis  they're also translucent which is always  a good sign so that  indicates that there's no fibrosis  of these spheres since you can still see  see through the capsules and so  then he also infused a large amount of  saline  and is going to aspirate uh the solution  um shortly and so this was to see  if any of the spheres had leaked out of  this uh  burst elementalist or this um kind of  tissue  uh membrane and so you can see we didn't  find uh any  any spheres um with them um within that  aspirated fluid so then we wanted to  look at the biocompatibility of the  spheres after retrieval  as we found very little fibrosis  in six out of seven of the nhp that were  transplanted  so these are images of inverted  phase contrast so any kind of dark  sphere  indicates that that that sphere was  basically completely covered  with fibrosis however we found that only  occurred in one of the nhp that was  retrieved at four months  then we took these retrieved spheres we  dissociated on these cells  on the surface and quantified the number  of  cd68 cd11d positive  macrophage cells which are important  kind of marker  important cell type that's involved in  the foreign body response to implantable  materials  and we can find a significantly reduced  uh  number of these cells compared to even  a plane alginate  control that contain no allogeneic  eyelets inside  and we estimated the number of viable  cells  per eyelet throughout the course of the  experiment from  pre-encapsulation all the way up to the  four-month  retrieved iowas and we find  that dy115 can protect  viable islets at a similar  comparatively high viability compared to  even before transplantation  then we stained the retrieved islets  with diffizone which is a red dye  that binds to zinc within mature insulin  granules and we can see  bright red staining indicating  mature and grafted mature endocrine  tissue  we then performed a glucose  glucose-stimulated insulin secretion  assay where you  incubate the retrieved ios in a low  glucose solution  for one hour and a high glucose solution  for one hour and we measure the amount  of insulin being secreted into the  supernatant  and we can find a significantly  increased amount of  insulin under high glucose compared to  low glucose  indicating that the iots are maintaining  their glucose responsiveness  um then we used a microfluidic-based  uh paraffusion assay to probe  some of the intracellular coupling  factors that are important  in the insulin secretion pathway  um so using a set of dyes we are able to  monitor  um basically the intracellular calcium  levels and then the mitochondrial  potential which are important um  pathways are in the influence secretion  pathway  so if you look at the center figure  maybe i'll go into detail  about how this pathway works so you have  glucose  come in it gets metabolized by  the mitochondria and converted to atp  this closes potassium ion dependent  or atp dependent potassium ion channels  this leads to a buildup of potassium  ions within  the cell that depolarizes the membrane  then this  opens up voltage dependent  calcium ion channels which causes an  influx  of calcium and then that influx of  calcium  causes mature insulin granules to bind  with the cell membrane for  insulin exocytosis and so here on the  top  right um you can see that once we  stimulate with a high glucose  at five minutes we get a spike of  intracellular calcium levels  that is not significantly different than  pre-transplant levels indicating that  these voltage-dependent  ion channels are intact within the  retrieve iowa  and similarly we see a similar decrease  in the mitochondrial potential in the  creating that  these mitochondria are actively engaging  in glycolysis  and important for in the insulin  succession pathway  so in this study we can show that the  long-term protection of viable and  glucose responses  l-genetic iots was achieved in non-human  primates without any immunosuppression  and from these and some other studies  that we've performed we have found  two small molecules that resist fibrosis  and we can graft these onto a variety of  materials and they have a similar effect  and these results initiated  the formation of a startup company  therapeutics in 2016 and they just have  recently transplanted the first patient  with an encapsulated engineered cell  product  that secretes factor eight for  hemophilia a which is exciting to see  and how material functions in human  patients  so i'd like to thank my advisors  dan and bob my grad school advisor host  jose oberhauser  all the other graduate students students  postdocs technicians  that have helped for these projects  and yeah normally i would take any  questions but i guess  maybe i will tomorrow

